WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.109
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.010
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.920
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.719
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:17.360
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:19.419
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.069
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.459 --> 00:00:24.209
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's the

00:00:24.209 --> 00:00:27.080
start of another three-part mini-series,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.770
this time on the great naval hero,

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:33.319
Horatio Nelson, Lady Hamilton, and the

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.810
love affair that scandalised Britain.

00:00:36.429 --> 00:00:39.169
In today's episode, we will follow

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:41.779
the life of Horatio Nelson, the

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.690
sickly clergyman's son who gave an

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.400
eye, an arm, and finally his life to

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.959
become Britain's greatest naval hero.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:54.729
In parts two and three, we'll tell his

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.199
story from the other side, from the

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:00.220
perspective of Emma Hamilton, the woman

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:03.709
born in poverty who reinvented herself and

00:01:03.709 --> 00:01:06.019
became the most famous woman in Europe.

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:08.459
And, yes, Nelson's lover.

00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:10.759
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:10.769 --> 00:01:13.039
minute and get right into it.

00:01:14.379 --> 00:01:16.569
There is an expression in English you may

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:19.909
know: "don't judge a book by its cover".

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:22.689
Most languages have a

00:01:22.699 --> 00:01:24.059
similar type of expression.

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:27.209
It means that appearance can be

00:01:27.389 --> 00:01:29.799
deceptive; just because someone

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:33.069
looks a certain way, their character

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.819
and behaviour may be different.

00:01:36.889 --> 00:01:40.219
And if you were to go to the village of

00:01:40.488 --> 00:01:43.559
Burnham Thorpe on the windswept coast

00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:46.309
of Norfolk, in the east of England, in

00:01:46.309 --> 00:01:50.499
the 1760s, and poke your nose into the

00:01:50.499 --> 00:01:53.459
vicar's house, you would find a boy.

00:01:53.929 --> 00:01:57.299
He was a small boy, and not a healthy one.

00:01:57.889 --> 00:01:59.859
All his life he would suffer

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:02.339
from seasickness, fevers,

00:02:02.709 --> 00:02:04.789
and a fragile constitution.

00:02:05.629 --> 00:02:08.139
If you lined him up next to the

00:02:08.189 --> 00:02:10.629
other boys in the village, and had

00:02:10.659 --> 00:02:13.709
to choose which one was most likely

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:16.779
to become not just a British war

00:02:16.779 --> 00:02:18.939
hero, but probably the greatest

00:02:18.949 --> 00:02:21.559
military leader in British history,

00:02:22.289 --> 00:02:24.049
you wouldn't have picked the small,

00:02:24.539 --> 00:02:27.409
ill-looking one called Horatio Nelson.

00:02:28.162 --> 00:02:31.299
But he had two things going for him.

00:02:32.039 --> 00:02:33.799
He had an uncle who was a

00:02:33.799 --> 00:02:35.219
captain in the Royal Navy.

00:02:35.759 --> 00:02:38.769
And he had, buried somewhere inside

00:02:38.769 --> 00:02:42.159
that small, sickly body, a limitless

00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:45.449
supply of ambition and courage.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.119
At the age of twelve, he asked

00:02:49.119 --> 00:02:50.629
to go to sea with his uncle.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:54.269
His uncle's reply has become famous.

00:02:54.719 --> 00:02:57.439
What has poor Horatio done, he

00:02:57.439 --> 00:03:00.329
asked, that he, so weak, should

00:03:00.339 --> 00:03:02.619
be sent to rough it out at sea?

00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:07.359
But he took the boy on, and Nelson joined

00:03:07.369 --> 00:03:10.199
the navy, and essentially never left.

00:03:11.479 --> 00:03:14.409
To his uncle's surprise, his

00:03:14.409 --> 00:03:17.279
nephew turned out to be fearless.

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:21.139
There is a story from an expedition to

00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:24.189
the Arctic, when Nelson was fifteen.

00:03:24.999 --> 00:03:27.379
One night, he slipped away from his

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:30.549
ship and walked out across the ice.

00:03:31.309 --> 00:03:33.939
He came across a polar bear that,

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:36.219
of course, tried to attack him.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:40.699
His gun misfired, so he turned it around

00:03:40.979 --> 00:03:43.009
and started beating the bear with the

00:03:43.009 --> 00:03:45.939
butt, the end you press on your shoulder.

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:48.739
When he was finally hauled back

00:03:48.749 --> 00:03:51.239
on board and asked what on earth

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:53.409
he had been thinking, he explained

00:03:53.709 --> 00:03:54.809
that he had wanted the bear's

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:57.069
skin, as a present for his father.

00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:00.329
The story may well have

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:01.759
grown in the telling.

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:03.799
But it was the kind of story

00:04:03.899 --> 00:04:05.799
people told about Nelson, and

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:08.659
would tell about him all his life.

00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:13.649
He rose astonishingly quickly, thanks

00:04:14.139 --> 00:04:15.859
partly to his uncle's connections

00:04:16.069 --> 00:04:18.649
and partly to sheer ability.

00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:22.139
He was a lieutenant at eighteen and a

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:24.819
captain at twenty, commanding ships and

00:04:24.839 --> 00:04:27.449
men while barely more than a boy himself.

00:04:28.576 --> 00:04:30.836
And curiously, for one of the greatest

00:04:30.836 --> 00:04:33.046
sailors who ever lived, he suffered

00:04:33.046 --> 00:04:35.716
from terrible seasickness all his life.

00:04:36.866 --> 00:04:39.026
He served in the West Indies, in

00:04:39.026 --> 00:04:40.736
the Caribbean, where he made himself

00:04:40.806 --> 00:04:43.606
unpopular by rigidly enforcing British

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.996
trade laws against American merchants.

00:04:46.436 --> 00:04:49.126
It was there, on the small island

00:04:49.136 --> 00:04:51.606
of Nevis, that he met a young

00:04:51.616 --> 00:04:53.976
widow called Frances Nisbet,

00:04:54.346 --> 00:04:56.276
known to everyone as Fanny.

00:04:57.036 --> 00:04:59.626
She was gentle, sensible,

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.086
and quickly devoted to him.

00:05:02.946 --> 00:05:07.316
They married in 1787, and the future King

00:05:07.376 --> 00:05:10.266
William the Fourth, then a young naval

00:05:10.266 --> 00:05:12.666
officer himself, gave the bride away.

00:05:13.636 --> 00:05:16.196
Fanny, for better or worse,

00:05:16.306 --> 00:05:17.816
will pop up throughout this

00:05:17.816 --> 00:05:20.306
mini-series, and unfortunately

00:05:20.426 --> 00:05:22.386
history has not been kind to her.

00:05:23.166 --> 00:05:26.196
So, Nelson is a newly-married

00:05:26.236 --> 00:05:28.566
rising star in the British navy,

00:05:28.906 --> 00:05:31.126
and an ambitious one at that.

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:34.206
But the problem was that there

00:05:34.206 --> 00:05:35.746
wasn't much fighting to be done.

00:05:36.346 --> 00:05:39.186
Britain was at peace, and a

00:05:39.186 --> 00:05:41.866
navy at peace has little use

00:05:41.876 --> 00:05:43.906
for ambitious young captains.

00:05:44.466 --> 00:05:47.916
Nelson was sent home on half pay, and

00:05:48.206 --> 00:05:50.476
he spent those years back in Norfolk

00:05:50.526 --> 00:05:52.986
with Fanny, digging his father's garden,

00:05:53.416 --> 00:05:55.666
arguing with the neighbours, and writing

00:05:55.666 --> 00:05:58.506
letters begging his superiors for a ship.

00:05:59.406 --> 00:06:02.986
And then, in 1793, revolutionary

00:06:02.996 --> 00:06:05.516
France declared war on Britain,

00:06:05.866 --> 00:06:08.066
and everything changed.

00:06:08.396 --> 00:06:11.616
Nelson got his ship, a sixty-four-gun

00:06:11.626 --> 00:06:14.796
warship called the Agamemnon, and he

00:06:14.796 --> 00:06:17.246
got a war that would last, with one

00:06:17.246 --> 00:06:20.146
short break, for the rest of his life.

00:06:21.686 --> 00:06:24.586
One of his first missions took him to the

00:06:24.586 --> 00:06:27.666
Bay of Naples, in the south of Italy, to

00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:30.306
ask the Kingdom of Naples for soldiers.

00:06:31.326 --> 00:06:32.726
He stayed a few days.

00:06:32.946 --> 00:06:35.366
He dined with the British ambassador,

00:06:35.766 --> 00:06:37.676
Sir William Hamilton, an old and

00:06:37.726 --> 00:06:39.706
cultured man he greatly admired.

00:06:40.136 --> 00:06:42.136
He also met the ambassador's

00:06:42.136 --> 00:06:44.356
wife, a dazzlingly beautiful

00:06:44.356 --> 00:06:46.456
young woman called Lady Hamilton.

00:06:46.996 --> 00:06:50.186
Now, her story is so colourful that

00:06:50.196 --> 00:06:52.196
the entire next two episodes will

00:06:52.196 --> 00:06:54.966
be dedicated to it, but for the

00:06:54.966 --> 00:06:57.316
purposes of today's episode, we just

00:06:57.316 --> 00:06:59.216
need to know that she was young,

00:06:59.706 --> 00:07:02.416
charming, but married to another man.

00:07:03.936 --> 00:07:06.806
Nelson wrote home to Fanny that he

00:07:06.806 --> 00:07:09.066
had had a satisfactory time in Naples,

00:07:09.316 --> 00:07:11.236
even describing the ambassador's wife

00:07:11.593 --> 00:07:14.296
as a young lady of "amiable manners

00:07:14.306 --> 00:07:15.676
who did honour to her station".

00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:19.876
And then he sailed away, and, to the best

00:07:19.876 --> 00:07:21.996
of our knowledge, thought little more

00:07:22.156 --> 00:07:24.206
about the beautiful ambassador's wife.

00:07:25.487 --> 00:07:28.237
Nelson spent the next years fighting the

00:07:28.237 --> 00:07:30.607
French up and down the Mediterranean,

00:07:30.977 --> 00:07:34.027
and the war began to consume him,

00:07:34.287 --> 00:07:36.667
quite literally, piece by piece.

00:07:37.927 --> 00:07:42.057
In 1794, during the siege of Calvi, on

00:07:42.057 --> 00:07:45.037
the island of Corsica, a French cannonball

00:07:45.347 --> 00:07:47.427
smashed into the ground in front of him

00:07:47.637 --> 00:07:50.807
and sprayed his face with sand and gravel.

00:07:51.557 --> 00:07:52.777
He lost most of the

00:07:52.777 --> 00:07:54.457
sight in his right eye.

00:07:55.357 --> 00:07:56.207
He shrugged it off.

00:07:56.897 --> 00:07:59.537
In his letters, he seemed almost proud

00:07:59.537 --> 00:08:02.351
of it, and he never wore an eyepatch.

00:08:03.567 --> 00:08:07.347
Then, in February 1797, came

00:08:07.347 --> 00:08:09.367
the battle that made his name.

00:08:10.627 --> 00:08:13.397
Off Cape St Vincent, on the coast of

00:08:13.397 --> 00:08:16.707
Portugal, a British fleet of fifteen

00:08:16.727 --> 00:08:20.257
ships met a Spanish fleet of twenty-seven.

00:08:21.487 --> 00:08:23.947
In the middle of the battle, Nelson

00:08:23.947 --> 00:08:26.217
saw that the Spanish ships were about

00:08:26.217 --> 00:08:28.727
to escape, and he did something that

00:08:28.727 --> 00:08:31.167
simply was not done in the Royal Navy.

00:08:32.227 --> 00:08:34.557
He pulled his ship out of the

00:08:34.557 --> 00:08:36.397
line of battle, without orders,

00:08:36.627 --> 00:08:38.857
and threw it directly into

00:08:38.857 --> 00:08:40.517
the path of the Spanish fleet.

00:08:41.537 --> 00:08:44.317
His ship was battered half to pieces.

00:08:44.717 --> 00:08:48.637
And then Nelson personally led a boarding

00:08:48.637 --> 00:08:51.917
party onto one enemy ship, captured

00:08:51.917 --> 00:08:55.017
it, and used it as a stepping stone to

00:08:55.017 --> 00:08:58.087
capture a second, larger one, scrambling

00:08:58.237 --> 00:09:00.947
from deck to deck with his sword drawn.

00:09:02.047 --> 00:09:04.157
For a captain to leave the line without

00:09:04.157 --> 00:09:07.217
orders was, technically, a court-martial

00:09:07.217 --> 00:09:09.487
offence, an offence that could have

00:09:09.487 --> 00:09:11.957
seen him put on trial by the navy.

00:09:12.937 --> 00:09:15.947
Instead, Britain made him a national

00:09:15.947 --> 00:09:19.027
hero, a knight, and an admiral.

00:09:20.077 --> 00:09:22.187
That was the thing about Nelson.

00:09:22.407 --> 00:09:24.707
He gambled, again and again,

00:09:24.837 --> 00:09:26.187
with his ships, with his

00:09:26.187 --> 00:09:28.097
men, and with his own body.

00:09:28.887 --> 00:09:31.767
And the gambles kept paying off.

00:09:32.347 --> 00:09:34.187
Well, not always…

00:09:35.457 --> 00:09:37.497
Five months later, he led a

00:09:37.497 --> 00:09:40.337
night attack on a Spanish port in

00:09:40.427 --> 00:09:42.767
Tenerife, in the Canary Islands.

00:09:43.477 --> 00:09:45.057
It was a disaster.

00:09:45.947 --> 00:09:48.247
As Nelson stepped out of his boat,

00:09:48.637 --> 00:09:51.538
a piece of grapeshot shattered

00:09:51.717 --> 00:09:53.547
his right arm above the elbow.

00:09:54.297 --> 00:09:56.517
The arm was amputated that

00:09:56.517 --> 00:09:58.667
night, by lamplight, in the

00:09:58.667 --> 00:10:00.227
surgeon's cabin of his ship.

00:10:01.037 --> 00:10:03.097
Within half an hour, it was said,

00:10:03.447 --> 00:10:05.447
Nelson was giving orders again.

00:10:06.407 --> 00:10:07.747
He came home to England

00:10:08.127 --> 00:10:11.097
depressed, in constant pain, and

00:10:11.107 --> 00:10:13.017
convinced his career was over.

00:10:13.567 --> 00:10:16.237
A one-eyed, one-armed admiral,

00:10:16.347 --> 00:10:17.757
he wrote, would never be

00:10:17.757 --> 00:10:19.177
useful to his country again.

00:10:20.307 --> 00:10:22.637
Back home, he was nursed back to

00:10:22.637 --> 00:10:24.937
health by his dutiful wife, Fanny,

00:10:25.107 --> 00:10:26.987
who patiently dressed the wound

00:10:27.027 --> 00:10:29.267
every day until it finally healed.

00:10:30.247 --> 00:10:32.417
And as for his prediction that

00:10:32.437 --> 00:10:34.967
his career was over, well, he

00:10:34.967 --> 00:10:36.497
couldn't have been more wrong.

00:10:37.677 --> 00:10:41.477
In the spring of 1798, a huge French

00:10:41.487 --> 00:10:44.457
fleet slipped out of the port of Toulon,

00:10:44.867 --> 00:10:47.407
carrying an army commanded by a young

00:10:47.407 --> 00:10:49.907
general called Napoleon Bonaparte.

00:10:51.247 --> 00:10:53.077
Nobody knew where it was going.

00:10:53.867 --> 00:10:56.427
Nelson was sent into the Mediterranean

00:10:56.427 --> 00:10:59.637
to find it, and for two exhausting

00:10:59.637 --> 00:11:01.727
months, he chased shadows.

00:11:02.437 --> 00:11:03.897
The French, it turned

00:11:03.897 --> 00:11:05.827
out, had gone to Egypt.

00:11:06.337 --> 00:11:08.157
Napoleon had a dream

00:11:08.297 --> 00:11:09.727
of conquering the East.

00:11:10.777 --> 00:11:13.597
And on the evening of the 1st of

00:11:13.597 --> 00:11:17.827
August, 1798, Nelson finally found

00:11:17.837 --> 00:11:20.157
the French fleet, anchored in a

00:11:20.187 --> 00:11:22.977
line at the mouth of the river Nile.

00:11:24.137 --> 00:11:25.897
It was late in the day.

00:11:26.377 --> 00:11:28.917
The safe, sensible choice was to

00:11:28.917 --> 00:11:31.787
wait until morning and then engage.

00:11:32.757 --> 00:11:35.527
Nelson attacked immediately.

00:11:36.237 --> 00:11:38.517
Some of his ships did something the

00:11:38.537 --> 00:11:41.347
French thought was impossible: they sailed

00:11:41.587 --> 00:11:44.347
between the anchored French ships and

00:11:44.347 --> 00:11:46.717
the shore, where there was barely enough

00:11:46.717 --> 00:11:49.277
water, so the French found themselves

00:11:49.327 --> 00:11:51.947
attacked from both sides at once.

00:11:53.327 --> 00:11:55.697
At around ten o'clock that night, the

00:11:55.967 --> 00:11:59.207
French flagship, a giant vessel called

00:11:59.257 --> 00:12:02.377
L'Orient, caught fire and exploded.

00:12:03.137 --> 00:12:05.607
The blast was so enormous that,

00:12:05.607 --> 00:12:07.457
for several minutes afterwards,

00:12:07.747 --> 00:12:09.657
the battle simply stopped.

00:12:10.197 --> 00:12:12.167
Sailors on both sides

00:12:12.377 --> 00:12:14.557
stood in silence, staring.

00:12:15.767 --> 00:12:19.017
By morning, the French Mediterranean

00:12:19.017 --> 00:12:21.257
fleet had ceased to exist.

00:12:22.167 --> 00:12:24.367
Napoleon's army was cut off,

00:12:24.657 --> 00:12:26.697
with no ships to take them home.

00:12:27.537 --> 00:12:29.417
It was one of the most complete

00:12:29.427 --> 00:12:31.827
victories in the history of naval

00:12:31.827 --> 00:12:35.187
warfare, and it turned Nelson from a

00:12:35.217 --> 00:12:38.857
British hero into a European celebrity.

00:12:39.607 --> 00:12:41.267
Medals were struck.

00:12:41.347 --> 00:12:42.237
Poems were written.

00:12:42.547 --> 00:12:45.467
The Sultan of the Ottoman Empire sent

00:12:45.467 --> 00:12:47.777
him a diamond ornament for his hat.

00:12:48.587 --> 00:12:50.617
Britain made him a baron:

00:12:51.037 --> 00:12:53.057
Lord Nelson of the Nile.

00:12:53.997 --> 00:12:55.117
He must have been thrilled,

00:12:55.407 --> 00:12:56.187
you might have thought.

00:12:57.017 --> 00:12:59.017
In fact, he was annoyed.

00:12:59.587 --> 00:13:01.747
A baron was the lowest rank

00:13:01.747 --> 00:13:04.417
of the aristocracy, and Nelson

00:13:04.687 --> 00:13:06.107
felt he deserved better.

00:13:07.137 --> 00:13:09.887
For all his brilliance, he had

00:13:09.887 --> 00:13:12.537
an almost childlike hunger for

00:13:12.537 --> 00:13:15.747
praise, for medals, for recognition.

00:13:16.887 --> 00:13:19.567
And part of it, to be fair, was

00:13:19.567 --> 00:13:21.477
about money as well as glory.

00:13:22.547 --> 00:13:25.427
Unlike other military leaders, Nelson

00:13:25.797 --> 00:13:28.227
had no great family fortune behind him;

00:13:28.717 --> 00:13:31.767
everything he had came from his pay and

00:13:31.767 --> 00:13:34.687
his prize money, and he never quite lost

00:13:34.697 --> 00:13:37.247
the feeling that his country rewarded

00:13:37.247 --> 00:13:39.887
him less generously than he deserved.

00:13:40.817 --> 00:13:44.097
Now, after the Battle of the Nile,

00:13:44.377 --> 00:13:48.097
Nelson was a hero, but physically,

00:13:48.337 --> 00:13:50.667
he was in a very bad way indeed.

00:13:51.407 --> 00:13:52.957
He had been wounded in the head

00:13:53.257 --> 00:13:55.327
during the battle, his ships were

00:13:55.427 --> 00:13:58.467
battered, and he was utterly exhausted.

00:13:59.547 --> 00:14:01.427
He needed a friendly port.

00:14:01.897 --> 00:14:05.537
And so, in September 1798, he

00:14:05.537 --> 00:14:08.337
sailed into the Bay of Naples.

00:14:09.667 --> 00:14:12.997
Waiting for him on the quayside was

00:14:13.257 --> 00:14:16.177
Emma, Lady Hamilton, the woman he had

00:14:16.177 --> 00:14:18.920
met for a few days five years earlier.

00:14:19.797 --> 00:14:22.437
She had organised a hero's welcome the

00:14:22.437 --> 00:14:25.297
likes of which even kings rarely received.

00:14:26.307 --> 00:14:28.817
And here the two stories

00:14:28.817 --> 00:14:30.137
of this mini-series meet.

00:14:31.207 --> 00:14:34.367
Emma had started life about as poor as

00:14:34.367 --> 00:14:36.897
it was possible to be, the daughter of a

00:14:36.917 --> 00:14:39.207
blacksmith who died when she was a baby.

00:14:40.127 --> 00:14:42.827
By 1798 she was the wife of the

00:14:42.837 --> 00:14:45.127
British ambassador, the closest friend

00:14:45.277 --> 00:14:47.927
of the Queen of Naples, and one of

00:14:47.937 --> 00:14:49.887
the most famous women in Europe.

00:14:51.047 --> 00:14:52.647
She had even played a part

00:14:52.867 --> 00:14:54.337
in Nelson's great victory.

00:14:55.417 --> 00:14:58.567
When his thirsty, hungry fleet needed

00:14:58.567 --> 00:15:01.847
supplies in Sicily, and neutral Naples

00:15:01.877 --> 00:15:04.307
could not officially help them, it

00:15:04.307 --> 00:15:06.037
was Emma's friendship with the Queen

00:15:06.337 --> 00:15:08.207
that helped quietly open the door.

00:15:09.437 --> 00:15:11.907
Nelson was dazzled by her.

00:15:12.437 --> 00:15:14.347
She was dazzled by him.

00:15:15.127 --> 00:15:18.667
And over the following two years, in

00:15:18.927 --> 00:15:21.567
Naples and then Sicily, they fell in

00:15:21.567 --> 00:15:26.567
love: he a married national hero, she

00:15:26.897 --> 00:15:29.397
the wife of the man he most admired.

00:15:30.637 --> 00:15:33.287
Nelson stayed in the Mediterranean far

00:15:33.287 --> 00:15:35.977
longer than he should have, his superiors

00:15:35.997 --> 00:15:38.547
in London grew more and more irritated,

00:15:38.887 --> 00:15:41.117
and when he finally came home to England

00:15:41.277 --> 00:15:44.447
in 1800, he did not come home to Fanny.

00:15:45.157 --> 00:15:48.097
He came home with the Hamiltons, with

00:15:48.337 --> 00:15:50.897
Sir William and the, shock horror,

00:15:51.377 --> 00:15:53.557
heavily pregnant Lady Hamilton.

00:15:54.757 --> 00:15:57.867
The father of the child was kept a secret,

00:15:58.317 --> 00:16:01.697
but the clue was in her name: Horatia.

00:16:02.967 --> 00:16:05.447
It was a scandal, a scandal

00:16:05.657 --> 00:16:06.967
we will go into in much more

00:16:06.967 --> 00:16:08.727
detail in parts two and three.

00:16:09.557 --> 00:16:12.602
The public adored him too much to care.

00:16:13.257 --> 00:16:16.477
The establishment cared very much indeed.

00:16:17.037 --> 00:16:19.447
And his private life had become

00:16:19.447 --> 00:16:21.457
the most famous scandal in Britain.

00:16:22.967 --> 00:16:25.067
Professionally, though, he remained

00:16:25.367 --> 00:16:28.857
untouchable, and in 1801 he proved

00:16:28.857 --> 00:16:31.407
it again at the Battle of Copenhagen,

00:16:31.777 --> 00:16:34.187
where Britain attacked the Danish fleet

00:16:34.547 --> 00:16:36.257
to break up an alliance of neutral

00:16:36.267 --> 00:16:38.567
countries that threatened British trade.

00:16:39.117 --> 00:16:42.737
It was a brutal, close-range fight, and

00:16:43.027 --> 00:16:45.927
halfway through, with the fighting at its

00:16:45.937 --> 00:16:48.737
fiercest, his commanding officer raised

00:16:48.757 --> 00:16:52.297
a signal flag ordering him to withdraw.

00:16:53.297 --> 00:16:57.277
Nelson ignored this order and, according

00:16:57.277 --> 00:16:59.497
to the story told afterwards, lifted

00:16:59.497 --> 00:17:01.967
his telescope to his blind eye.

00:17:03.037 --> 00:17:05.257
"I really do not see the signal", he said.

00:17:06.687 --> 00:17:08.817
He fought on, and he won.

00:17:10.377 --> 00:17:13.757
By 1805, Napoleon ruled most of

00:17:13.757 --> 00:17:16.417
Europe and was preparing to invade

00:17:16.427 --> 00:17:18.487
Britain, or at least, that's what

00:17:18.517 --> 00:17:20.237
most people in Britain thought.

00:17:21.197 --> 00:17:23.507
Only one thing stood in

00:17:23.507 --> 00:17:25.787
his way: the Royal Navy.

00:17:26.797 --> 00:17:30.207
And in October of that year, the combined

00:17:30.217 --> 00:17:33.487
fleets of France and Spain came out of

00:17:33.487 --> 00:17:36.077
the port of Cadiz, in southern Spain.

00:17:36.957 --> 00:17:39.597
Their fleet was a combined thirty-three

00:17:39.597 --> 00:17:46.717
ships, almost 30,000 men, and 2,600 guns.

00:17:48.067 --> 00:17:50.607
Off a cape in southwest Spain called

00:17:50.657 --> 00:17:53.717
Trafalgar, Nelson was waiting for them.

00:17:54.697 --> 00:17:57.447
His fleet had twenty-seven ships,

00:17:58.057 --> 00:18:02.177
17,000 men and just over 2,000 guns.

00:18:03.407 --> 00:18:06.857
On paper, the numbers favoured the enemy.

00:18:07.977 --> 00:18:10.257
In reality, almost everything

00:18:10.257 --> 00:18:11.427
else favoured Britain.

00:18:12.487 --> 00:18:15.697
The Royal Navy of 1805 was probably

00:18:15.717 --> 00:18:18.397
the finest fighting force in the world.

00:18:19.017 --> 00:18:21.697
Years of blockade duty meant that British

00:18:21.697 --> 00:18:24.877
crews were constantly at sea, sailing and

00:18:25.107 --> 00:18:27.997
drilling in all weathers, while the French

00:18:28.017 --> 00:18:31.167
and Spanish crews largely sat in port.

00:18:32.367 --> 00:18:34.827
A well-trained British gun crew could

00:18:34.827 --> 00:18:38.507
fire two or even three times in the time

00:18:38.507 --> 00:18:40.747
it took their opponents to fire one.

00:18:42.007 --> 00:18:44.227
Nelson knew all this.

00:18:44.517 --> 00:18:45.997
It was the foundation that

00:18:46.057 --> 00:18:47.377
everything else was built on.

00:18:48.327 --> 00:18:50.717
As the fleets closed in on each other,

00:18:51.137 --> 00:18:53.657
on the morning of the 21st of October,

00:18:54.117 --> 00:18:56.787
he ordered a signal to be run up the

00:18:56.787 --> 00:18:59.987
masts of his flagship, HMS Victory, a

00:18:59.987 --> 00:19:02.777
message to every sailor in the fleet.

00:19:04.157 --> 00:19:06.427
England expects that every

00:19:06.427 --> 00:19:08.537
man will do his duty.

00:19:09.527 --> 00:19:11.177
The men cheered.

00:19:11.907 --> 00:19:14.567
Nelson had that effect, it seemed,

00:19:14.917 --> 00:19:16.607
on the people who served under him.

00:19:17.167 --> 00:19:19.367
His captains called themselves his

00:19:19.377 --> 00:19:22.257
band of brothers, and sailors who had

00:19:22.257 --> 00:19:24.377
never exchanged a word with him would

00:19:24.387 --> 00:19:26.247
boast, for the rest of their lives,

00:19:26.507 --> 00:19:28.847
that they had fought alongside Nelson.

00:19:29.827 --> 00:19:33.027
Now, I don't want to get too bogged down

00:19:33.267 --> 00:19:35.877
in the intricacies of these naval battles,

00:19:36.077 --> 00:19:38.677
but it is important to understand a

00:19:38.677 --> 00:19:41.397
little bit about how they tended to work.

00:19:42.767 --> 00:19:45.357
These ships were, essentially,

00:19:45.697 --> 00:19:48.287
huge floating fortresses.

00:19:49.227 --> 00:19:51.707
Most battles would involve the ships

00:19:51.937 --> 00:19:54.927
lining up against each other and blasting

00:19:54.927 --> 00:19:56.937
each other with cannon shot from close

00:19:56.937 --> 00:19:59.397
distance until one side surrendered.

00:20:01.137 --> 00:20:02.567
Nelson's plan for this

00:20:02.587 --> 00:20:04.187
battle was different.

00:20:05.417 --> 00:20:07.737
Instead of forming a traditional

00:20:07.747 --> 00:20:11.277
line, his ships would sail straight

00:20:11.307 --> 00:20:14.267
at the enemy in two columns and

00:20:14.297 --> 00:20:16.447
smash their line into pieces.

00:20:17.867 --> 00:20:20.367
This would mean they would be forced to

00:20:20.367 --> 00:20:23.297
accept terrible damage on the attack,

00:20:23.657 --> 00:20:26.117
because the ships could only fire side-on.

00:20:26.687 --> 00:20:28.967
But once they broke through,

00:20:29.487 --> 00:20:31.297
it would do three things.

00:20:32.277 --> 00:20:34.607
Firstly, it would cut the

00:20:34.609 --> 00:20:37.327
Franco-Spanish fleet in half, allowing

00:20:37.377 --> 00:20:39.607
the British to focus their attack.

00:20:40.567 --> 00:20:44.197
Secondly, by firing from a ninety degree

00:20:44.247 --> 00:20:47.677
angle, as opposed to side by side, the

00:20:47.687 --> 00:20:50.517
British ships could fire their cannons at

00:20:50.537 --> 00:20:53.917
the poorly protected fronts and backs of

00:20:53.917 --> 00:20:56.627
the enemy ships, with cannonballs smashing

00:20:56.627 --> 00:20:58.797
down the whole length of their gun decks.

00:20:59.807 --> 00:21:02.427
And thirdly, it would create an

00:21:02.467 --> 00:21:04.737
atmosphere of chaos which gave

00:21:04.747 --> 00:21:06.957
the British a serious advantage.

00:21:07.647 --> 00:21:09.707
The British sailors were better

00:21:09.707 --> 00:21:11.657
trained, they could reload and

00:21:11.657 --> 00:21:14.177
fire faster, and in a state of

00:21:14.207 --> 00:21:16.957
confusion, they had the upper hand.

00:21:18.377 --> 00:21:21.007
Nelson, of course, would lead

00:21:21.027 --> 00:21:22.727
one of the ships that would sail

00:21:22.757 --> 00:21:25.097
straight at the enemy, straight into

00:21:25.097 --> 00:21:27.917
a hail of bullets and cannon fire.

00:21:29.537 --> 00:21:31.487
To state the obvious, it was an

00:21:31.487 --> 00:21:34.327
incredibly dangerous strategy, and

00:21:34.537 --> 00:21:36.567
Nelson knew that his chances of

00:21:36.567 --> 00:21:38.607
surviving the battle were slim.

00:21:39.877 --> 00:21:42.577
This was especially so because

00:21:42.597 --> 00:21:45.087
officers did not duck down.

00:21:46.397 --> 00:21:48.237
Ordinary sailors could at least

00:21:48.287 --> 00:21:50.167
crouch behind the ship's wooden

00:21:50.167 --> 00:21:52.077
sides when the fire grew heavy.

00:21:52.767 --> 00:21:55.777
Officers were expected not to do this.

00:21:56.457 --> 00:21:59.627
Custom demanded that a commander stand

00:21:59.687 --> 00:22:02.727
upright on the open deck, calm and

00:22:02.727 --> 00:22:05.147
apparently unbothered, while the air

00:22:05.187 --> 00:22:07.637
around him filled with flying metal.

00:22:08.827 --> 00:22:10.757
This required almost

00:22:11.147 --> 00:22:12.707
indescribable bravery.

00:22:13.597 --> 00:22:16.547
And Nelson, Britain's most famous

00:22:16.577 --> 00:22:19.177
naval commander, was the epitome,

00:22:19.177 --> 00:22:21.167
the perfect example, of this.

00:22:22.227 --> 00:22:24.187
He never sheltered below deck in his

00:22:24.187 --> 00:22:27.297
cabin; he would stand on deck in his

00:22:27.297 --> 00:22:29.787
finest uniform, wearing every star

00:22:29.787 --> 00:22:32.247
and medal he owned, as the cannonballs

00:22:32.467 --> 00:22:34.187
began to strike home around him.

00:22:35.277 --> 00:22:38.157
And this was what he did at Trafalgar,

00:22:38.417 --> 00:22:40.967
facing the full might of the superior

00:22:40.967 --> 00:22:43.507
in numbers Franco-Spanish fleet.

00:22:44.847 --> 00:22:48.267
But this time, lady luck was not with him.

00:22:49.487 --> 00:22:51.347
And at the height of the battle, as

00:22:51.357 --> 00:22:53.857
the Victory lay locked against a French

00:22:53.857 --> 00:22:56.947
ship, a marksman in the enemy rigging

00:22:57.337 --> 00:22:59.538
looked down at the British quarterdeck

00:22:59.937 --> 00:23:03.047
and, through the smoke, saw a small

00:23:03.047 --> 00:23:05.857
man in an admiral's coat, glittering

00:23:06.037 --> 00:23:08.567
with stars and medals, medals his

00:23:08.597 --> 00:23:10.747
officers had advised him not to wear

00:23:11.057 --> 00:23:13.207
so as not to draw attention to himself.

00:23:14.245 --> 00:23:16.037
The Frenchman fired.

00:23:16.647 --> 00:23:19.467
The bullet struck Nelson in the shoulder

00:23:19.837 --> 00:23:22.497
and passed down through his spine.

00:23:24.017 --> 00:23:26.167
He was carried below decks, to the

00:23:26.167 --> 00:23:28.457
dark, crowded space beneath the

00:23:28.457 --> 00:23:30.177
waterline where the wounded lay.

00:23:31.437 --> 00:23:33.567
He told the doctor, "You can do

00:23:33.567 --> 00:23:35.417
nothing for me. I have but a short

00:23:35.417 --> 00:23:37.807
time to live. My back is shot through".

00:23:39.117 --> 00:23:41.227
The doctor could not disagree.

00:23:42.237 --> 00:23:45.017
Nelson was dying, but he clung

00:23:45.027 --> 00:23:47.337
onto life for another three hours,

00:23:47.527 --> 00:23:49.697
as if he could not die before

00:23:49.697 --> 00:23:51.177
learning the result of the battle.

00:23:52.327 --> 00:23:53.907
When he heard the cheers of his

00:23:53.907 --> 00:23:56.467
sailors above deck, he knew that

00:23:56.467 --> 00:23:58.677
his strategy had worked perfectly.

00:23:59.757 --> 00:24:01.777
Of the thirty-three French

00:24:01.777 --> 00:24:03.857
and Spanish ships, eighteen

00:24:04.047 --> 00:24:05.577
were captured or destroyed.

00:24:06.267 --> 00:24:07.947
Britain lost none.

00:24:09.277 --> 00:24:11.177
He asked his old friend Captain

00:24:11.177 --> 00:24:13.607
Hardy to kiss him, and Hardy

00:24:13.627 --> 00:24:15.847
knelt down and kissed his cheek.

00:24:16.747 --> 00:24:19.027
Among his final words was the simple

00:24:19.027 --> 00:24:22.327
sentence: thank God, I have done my duty.

00:24:23.427 --> 00:24:24.827
Britain gave Nelson the

00:24:24.827 --> 00:24:26.587
greatest funeral in its history.

00:24:27.327 --> 00:24:29.777
His body was brought home preserved

00:24:29.797 --> 00:24:32.417
in a barrel of brandy, and tens of

00:24:32.417 --> 00:24:34.397
thousands of people lined the streets

00:24:34.397 --> 00:24:37.007
of London in silence as the coffin

00:24:37.017 --> 00:24:38.937
was carried to St Paul's Cathedral.

00:24:40.297 --> 00:24:43.027
And the mourning never really stopped.

00:24:43.717 --> 00:24:45.757
If you go to London today, you can

00:24:45.757 --> 00:24:48.417
stand in a square named after his

00:24:48.417 --> 00:24:50.767
final battle: Trafalgar Square,

00:24:51.107 --> 00:24:52.937
right in the centre of the city.

00:24:53.977 --> 00:24:56.047
In the middle of it, on top of a

00:24:56.067 --> 00:24:58.757
column more than fifty metres tall,

00:24:59.137 --> 00:25:01.927
stands a statue of a small, one-armed

00:25:01.947 --> 00:25:04.217
man, gazing out towards the sea.

00:25:05.247 --> 00:25:07.437
That man is Horatio Nelson.

00:25:09.677 --> 00:25:11.487
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:25:11.487 --> 00:25:14.227
episode — the first part of our three-part

00:25:14.227 --> 00:25:16.337
series on Nelson and Lady Hamilton.

00:25:16.637 --> 00:25:19.597
Next up, in parts two and three, we will

00:25:19.597 --> 00:25:21.747
be telling the amazing story of Emma

00:25:21.757 --> 00:25:24.627
Hamilton, the woman who rose from poverty

00:25:24.877 --> 00:25:27.027
to become an ambassador's wife, the most

00:25:27.027 --> 00:25:29.417
famous woman in Europe, and yes, the

00:25:29.417 --> 00:25:31.757
lover of Britain's greatest naval hero.

00:25:32.446 --> 00:25:34.276
. 
You've been listening to English Learning

00:25:34.276 --> 00:25:36.976
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:25:37.416 --> 00:25:40.256
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:25:40.266 --> 00:25:42.596
I'll catch you in the next episode.

